tel 1.0

base wisteria
import soundness

info   generic derivation of typeclass instances for products and sums, from two rules written once
detail
    _Wisteria_ is the derivation engine behind every derived codec in Soundness — the JSON
    encoders built from case classes, the CSV rows built from fields, the digests built from
    structures. A typeclass author writes two rules, `conjunction` (how the instances of a
    **product**'s fields combine into an instance for the product) and `disjunction` (how an
    instance for a **sum** chooses among its variants), by extending `Derivation`, and every
    case class, tuple, enumeration and sealed trait then has an instance, resolved through the
    inherited `derived` given or requested with a `derives` clause. There is no macro to write:
    the two rules are ordinary inline methods, and the field-by-field or variant-by-variant
    work is done by the inherited combinators `fields`, `contexts`, `build`, `construct`,
    `variant`, `delegate`, `choices` and `singleton`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import dysasymptotics.linearAccess
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    trait Presentation[value]:
      def present(value: value): Text

    object Presentation extends Derivation[Presentation]:
      given Presentation[Text] = identity(_)
      given Presentation[Int] = _.toString.tt

      inline def conjunction[derivation <: Product: ProductReflection]: Presentation[derivation] =
        value =>
          fields(value):
            [field] => field => t"$label=${contextual.present(field)}"
          . join(t"${typeName[derivation]}(", t", ", t")")

      inline def disjunction[derivation: SumReflection]: Presentation[derivation] =
        value =>
          variant(value):
            [variant <: derivation] => variant => contextual.present(variant)

    extension [value](value: value)
      def present(using presentation: Presentation[value]): Text = presentation.present(value)

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int) derives Presentation

    Person(t"Hamlet", 30).present   // t"Person(name=Hamlet, age=30)"
    ```

    Inside the polymorphic lambda each combinator takes, the field or variant is described by
    a handful of contextual values, read through the package's accessors: `contextual` is the
    typeclass instance for the field's own type, `label` its name as written, `index` its
    position, `default` (from vacuous) the field's declared default value or `Unset`, and
    `dereference` a function from the product to the field. The `[field] => field => …`
    syntax is a polymorphic lambda: every field has a different type, and the body knows only
    that `contextual` is an instance for precisely that type, which is all it needs.

    A **product** is a case class, a tuple, an enumeration case or a singleton (a case object,
    or a parameterless enumeration case, which is a product with no fields); its values are
    **fields**, with fixed types, a canonical order and labels. A **sum** is an enumeration or
    a sealed trait; its alternatives are **variants**. The engine recognises each through
    `scala.deriving.Mirror`, aliased here as `Reflection`, `ProductReflection` and
    `SumReflection`, so a type with no mirror — a plain trait, a class which is not a case
    class — fails at the point of derivation.

    `Derivation` derives for both products and sums; `ProductDerivation` for products alone,
    so that applying the typeclass to a sum is a compile error, and `SumDerivation` for sums
    alone. For a typeclass written in the `Self` style (`value is Display`), the aliases
    `Derivable` and `ProductDerivable` adapt the shape. A derived instance sits at lower
    priority than any given written in the companion's own body, so a hand-written instance
    for one type always wins. The derivation is invisible to the user of a typeclass, for whom
    defining a case class is enough.

    The engine derives a whole **graph** at once: `derived[T]` visits every type reachable from
    `T`'s fields and variants, emits one instance per distinct type, and resolves each field
    through the real implicit search while ignoring the typeclass's own derivation givens, so
    a field's instance is a hand-written one, a codec for a collection, or a sibling in the
    same graph — and a recursive type resolves to itself rather than re-entering the
    derivation. A recursive type is still best given its instance on its companion with a
    `derives` clause.

    Beyond codecs, the `arithmetic` object derives `Addable`, `Subtractable`, `Multiplicable`
    and `Divisible` field-wise for any product, each a given imported by name:
    `import arithmetic.addable`. Per-field overrides come from vicarious: a `derivation is
    Specific over typeclass` in scope, built with `specifically`, supplies a different instance
    at a named field path, and every combinator consults it.

    The library is one source set, `wisteria.core`, which compiles for the JVM, Scala.js and
    Scala Native. Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, with one caveat: the
    accessor `label` shares its name with tarantula's `Element#label()`, so a file which
    imports both and sees `label` reported ambiguous should write `wisteria.label`.

refer wisteria.Derivation
refer wisteria.ProductDerivation
refer wisteria.SumDerivation
refer wisteria.Variant
refer vicarious.Specific

entry .Derivation   the base of a typeclass companion which derives for both products and sums
  detail
      `object Typeclass extends Derivation[Typeclass]` gives the typeclass a derivation: the
      object implements `conjunction` (the product rule, from `ProductDerivation.Methods`) and
      `disjunction` (the sum rule, from `SumDerivation.Methods`), and inherits `derived`, the
      given through which any product or sum type resolves an instance. The type parameter
      is the typeclass's type constructor; a `Self`-style typeclass uses `Derivable` instead.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dysasymptotics.linearAccess

      trait Equivalence[value]:
        def equal(left: value, right: value): Boolean

      object Equivalence extends Derivation[Equivalence]:
        given Equivalence[Int] = _ == _
        given Equivalence[Text] = _.lower == _.lower

        inline def conjunction[derivation <: Product: ProductReflection]: Equivalence[derivation] =
          (left, right) =>
            contexts[derivation]():
              [field] => equivalence =>
                val extract: derivation => field = dereference
                equivalence.equal(extract(left), extract(right))
            . all { boolean => boolean }

        inline def disjunction[derivation: SumReflection]: Equivalence[derivation] =
          (left, right) =>
            variant(left):
              [variant <: derivation] => leftValue =>
                complement(right).lay(false)(contextual.equal(leftValue, _))

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      Equivalence.derived[Person].equal(Person(t"Hamlet", 30), Person(t"HAMLET", 30))   // true
      ```

      Both rules are `inline`: they expand at the use site, once per type in the derived
      graph, which is what lets the lambda's `contextual` be the instance for the right field
      type and lets `inline if choice[derivation]` reject a shape at compile time with
      `scala.compiletime.error` (written fully qualified). Two mistakes are common enough to
      name: a long error ending _given instance derived in trait Derivation does not match
      type…_ almost always means a polymorphic lambda's type variable lacks its upper bound
      (`[variant <: derivation]`, not `[variant]`), and a mismatch against `Product` means
      `conjunction`'s type parameter lacks `<: Product`.

      The object need not be the typeclass's companion. Defined on an ordinary object, the
      rules give derivation without exposing it to implicit search, and `Rules.derived[T]` is
      called wherever an instance is wanted — the way to offer two derivations of one typeclass,
      a strict and a lenient parser, say.

      Under capture checking, a rule's result type is `typeclass[derivation]^`: a derived
      instance may honestly capture the capabilities its resolution needed, such as a decoder's
      tactic, and the capture is erased once, in `derivedOne`. A derivation expands in the
      module which uses it, so a capture error in a derivation is reported in that module, not
      the typeclass's own. A sum rule which calls `delegate` or `singleton` needs a
      `Tactic[Variant.Error]` where it expands; the usual shape seals it inside the instance
      with `provide[Tactic[Variant.Error]]`, so that the derived instance is not itself a
      capability.
  refer wisteria.ProductDerivation.Methods
  refer wisteria.SumDerivation.Methods
  refer wisteria.Derivable
  entry #derivedOne   an instance for one type, product or sum, one level deep
      Chooses `disjunction` for a sum and `conjunction` for a product, by inspecting the type's
      symbol, and narrows the fresh result to `typeclass[derivation]`. Its fields and variants
      resolve to sibling instances; `derived` calls it once for every distinct type in the
      graph, and a derivation reaches it directly when it wants exactly one level.
  entry #derived   the given which derives an instance for any type with a `Reflection`
      `derived[derivation]`, taking a `Reflection[derivation]` (a `scala.deriving.Mirror`), is
      the given implicit search finds for every product and sum type, and the method a
      `derives Typeclass` clause names. It builds the whole graph of types reachable from
      `derivation` — every field type and variant type, recursively — emits one lazily
      bound instance per distinct type, and returns the root's. A field whose type has a
      hand-written given, or a codec such as `List[T]`'s, uses that; a field whose type is
      another product or sum in the graph uses its sibling; a type which reaches itself
      resolves to its own binding. A field type with no instance at all is a compile error at
      the derivation, and compiling an explicit `Typeclass.derived[T]` at the call site is the
      quickest way to learn which field is missing one.

entry .ProductDerivation   the base of a typeclass companion which derives for products only
  detail
      `object Typeclass extends ProductDerivation[Typeclass]` gives the typeclass a derivation
      for case classes, tuples and singletons, and none for sums: applying it to an
      enumeration or a sealed trait is a compile error naming the mismatch rather than a
      runtime surprise. The object implements `conjunction` alone, and inherits `derived`. The
      combinators a product rule is written with — `fields`, `contexts`, `build`, `construct`,
      `complement`, `typeName`, `tuple` and `singleton` — are the members of
      `ProductDerivation.Methods`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dysasymptotics.linearAccess

      trait Parsing[value]:
        def parse(text: Text): value

      object Parsing extends ProductDerivation[Parsing]:
        given Parsing[Text] = identity(_)
        given Parsing[Int] = _.s.toInt

        inline def conjunction[derivation <: Product: ProductReflection]: Parsing[derivation] =
          text =>
            val columns = text.cut(t",")
            build[derivation]:
              [field] => parsing => parsing.parse(columns(Ordinal.zerary(index)).or(t""))

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      Parsing.derived[Person].parse(t"Hamlet,30")   // Person(t"Hamlet", 30)
      ```
  refer wisteria.Derivation
  refer wisteria.ProductDerivable
  entry .Methods   the product combinators: the vocabulary a `conjunction` is written in
    detail
        The trait every `Derivation` and `ProductDerivation` mixes in. It declares the
        abstract product rule, `conjunction`, and supplies the inline combinators the rule is
        written with. Each takes a polymorphic lambda, `[field] => … => result`, and expands
        it once per field, with the field's contextual values in scope: `contextual`, the
        `typeclass[field]` instance; `label`, the field's name as `Text`; `index`, its position
        as `Int & FieldIndex[field]`; `default`, a `Default[Optional[field]]` holding the
        declared default or `Unset`; and, in `contexts`, `dereference`, a function
        `derivation -> field`. The combinators are `protected`, so they are reached from inside
        the deriving object:

        - `fields(value)(lambda)` folds over the fields of a value, giving the lambda each
          field's value, and returns the results as a frozen `Array[result]`. This is the
          **consumer**'s combinator: a renderer, an encoder, a hash. The lambda's parameter is
          the field's value.
        - `contexts[derivation]()(lambda)` is the same fold with no value: the lambda's
          parameter is the field's instance, and `dereference` is available for reaching into a
          value — or two values, which is how an equivalence compares corresponding fields
          without separating them from the lambda that typed them. For a schema, or the list
          of a product's labels, no value is needed at all.
        - `build[derivation](lambda)` constructs a new value of the product: the lambda's
          parameter is the field's instance, and it returns the field's value. This is the
          **producer**'s combinator: a parser, a decoder, a generator. Construction goes
          through the mirror's `fromProduct`, which is what makes it work for case classes
          nested in objects and declared inside methods.
        - `construct[constructor, derivation](bind, pure, lambda)` is `build` for a lambda
          whose result is wrapped in a type constructor — an `Optional`, an `Attempt`, a
          `List` — threading the fields through the polymorphic `bind` and `pure` it is given,
          so that a failing field fails the product:
          `construct[Option, derivation]([in, out] => _.flatMap, [monadic] => Some(_), lambda)`.
        - `complement(product)` reads the current field out of another value of the same
          product, typed with the field's type, inside any of the lambdas; `dereference` is the
          same access as a function.

        A fold summons the typeclass for **every** field, so even a typeclass whose leaf
        instances are trivial (the labels of a `Text`, say) must provide them. `index` is a
        branded `Int`, so a native `List` is indexed with `Ordinal.zerary(index)` and yields
        an `Optional`; the complexity gate for indexing (`dysasymptotics.linearAccess`) must be
        imported where the derivation expands. Under capture checking, a value a lambda reads
        from an array-typed field keeps the field's capture annotation, so a product with a
        frozen `Array[Long]^{}` field derives cleanly.

        Because the rule is `inline`, a gated collection operation inside it — `.all`,
        `.to[List]`, indexing a `List` by an ordinal — is resolved where the derivation
        expands, against everything imported there. If such an operation fails to resolve in
        the expansion though the same line compiles in a plain `def`, move it into an ordinary
        helper method which the rule calls.
    entry #conjunction   the product rule: an instance for a product from its fields' instances
        Abstract, implemented by the deriving object. `conjunction[derivation <: Product:
        ProductReflection]: typeclass[derivation]^` is called once per product type in a
        derived graph, with the mirror in scope, and returns the instance, usually as a lambda
        over `fields`, `contexts`, `build` or `construct`. The `<: Product` bound and the
        context bound are both required. The result is fresh (`^`) under capture checking, so
        the instance may capture what its construction needed; `Derivation#derivedOne` erases
        the capture.
    entry #typeName   the name of the type being derived for, as `Text`
        `typeName[derivation]` is the mirror's label: `Person` for `case class Person`, `Tuple2`
        for a pair. Available in both rules, and in the lambdas, for a rendering which names
        the type or a variant.
    entry #tuple   whether the product is a tuple, decided as the code compiles
        `inline if tuple[derivation] then … else …` lets a rendering drop the type name for a
        tuple, whose fields are labelled `_1`, `_2`, ….
    entry #singleton   whether the product is a singleton — a case object or a parameterless case
        `inline if singleton[derivation] then typeName[derivation] else …`: a singleton has no
        fields, so a rendering shows only its name, and a parser has nothing to parse.
  entry #derivedOne   an instance for one product type, one level deep
      `conjunction` applied to the type, with its mirror summoned, and the fresh result
      narrowed to `typeclass[derivation]`. A sum type is a compile error here.
  entry #derived   the given which derives an instance for any product type
      As `Derivation#derived`: builds the graph of reachable types and returns the root's
      instance, each type derived with `derivedOne`. A sum among the reachable types is a
      compile error, since this derivation has no `disjunction`.

entry .SumDerivation   the base of a typeclass companion which derives for sums only
  detail
      `object Typeclass extends SumDerivation[Typeclass]` gives the typeclass a derivation for
      enumerations and sealed traits, and none for products: the variants' instances must come
      from elsewhere, hand-written givens as a rule. The object implements `disjunction`
      alone, and inherits `derived`. The combinators a sum rule is written with — `variant`,
      `delegate`, `choices`, `singleton`, `complement`, `variantLabels` and `choice` — are the
      members of `SumDerivation.Methods`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      trait Handling[value]:
        def handle(value: value): Unit

      enum Signal:
        case Start(at: Int)
        case Stop(reason: Text)

      object Handling extends SumDerivation[Handling]:
        given Handling[Signal.Start] = start => ()
        given Handling[Signal.Stop] = stop => ()

        inline def disjunction[derivation: SumReflection]: Handling[derivation] =
          value => variant(value):
            [variant <: derivation] => value => contextual.handle(value)

      summon[Handling[Signal]].handle(Signal.Stop(t"done"))
      ```
  refer wisteria.Derivation
  refer wisteria.Variant
  entry .Methods   the sum combinators: the vocabulary a `disjunction` is written in
    detail
        The trait every `Derivation` and `SumDerivation` mixes in. It declares the abstract
        sum rule, `disjunction`, and supplies the inline combinators the rule is written with.
        Each takes a polymorphic lambda over the variant, `[variant <: derivation] => … =>
        result` — the upper bound is required — and expands it with the variant's contextual
        values in scope: `contextual`, the `typeclass[variant]` instance; `label`, the
        variant's name as `Text`; and `index`, its position as `Int & Variant.Index[variant]`.
        The combinators are `protected`, so they are reached from inside the deriving object:

        - `variant(sum)(lambda)` dispatches on the actual case of a value: the lambda's
          parameter is the value narrowed to its variant, so the body is typed at the case
          rather than at the sum. This is the **consumer**'s combinator.
        - `delegate(label)(lambda)` is told which variant to produce, by its label, and gives
          the lambda that variant's instance; this is the **producer**'s combinator, for a
          decoder whose input carries a discriminator. A label naming no variant raises
          `Variant.Error`, so a `Tactic[Variant.Error]` must be in scope where the rule
          expands — sealed inside the instance with `provide[Tactic[Variant.Error]]` as a rule.
        - `singleton(label)` turns a label back into the value it names, for a sum whose
          variants are all singletons; an unknown label raises `Variant.Error` likewise.
        - `choices(lambda)` folds over every variant with no value to dispatch on, giving the
          lambda each variant's instance, label and index, and returns a frozen
          `Array[result]`: what a derivation producing a schema rather than an instance needs.
        - `complement(sum)` reads the other value of a two-value typeclass narrowed to the
          current variant, as an `Optional`, since the two need not be the same variant.
        - `variantLabels` lists the variants' labels, in declaration order.

        `choice[derivation]` reports, as the code compiles, whether every variant is a
        singleton — an enumeration of names, with no data — and is written `inline if
        choice[derivation] then … else scala.compiletime.error("…")` so that a derivation which
        makes sense only for such enumerations rejects the rest with its own message.
    entry #disjunction   the sum rule: an instance for a sum from its variants' instances
        Abstract, implemented by the deriving object. `disjunction[derivation:
        SumReflection]: typeclass[derivation]^` is called once per sum type in a derived graph,
        with the mirror in scope, and returns the instance, usually as a lambda over `variant`
        or `delegate`. Fresh (`^`) under capture checking, like `conjunction`.
    entry #choice   whether every variant of the sum is a singleton, decided as the code compiles
        `transparent inline`, so `inline if choice[derivation]` is resolved at compile time
        and the branch not taken is eliminated — including a `scala.compiletime.error` which
        would otherwise fail the build. True for an enumeration of bare names and a sealed
        trait of case objects; false as soon as one variant carries data. Decided by inspecting
        the type's symbol, so it is reliable deep inside the graph's inlining.
  entry #derivedOne   an instance for one sum type, one level deep
      `disjunction` applied to the type, with its mirror summoned, and the fresh result
      narrowed to `typeclass[derivation]`. A product type is a compile error here.
  entry #derived   the given which derives an instance for any sum type
      As `Derivation#derived`: builds the graph of reachable types and returns the root's
      instance. A product among the reachable types — a variant with fields, say — must have
      an instance of its own, since this derivation has no `conjunction`.

entry .Derivable   `Derivation` for a typeclass written in the `Self` style: `value is Display`
  detail
      `Derivation` takes a type constructor, `Typeclass[_]`. A typeclass whose subject is a
      `Self` type member — `trait Display { type Self; def display(value: Self): Text }`, used
      as `Text is Display` — has no type constructor to give it, so `Derivable[Display]`
      spells one: it is `Derivation[[self] =>> Display { type Self = self }]`. The rules are
      then written with the `is` syntax in their result types, and the same combinators apply.

      Watched as a typeclass grows: a `Display` first written for products alone, with
      `ProductDerivable`, then given a sum rule as well, with `Derivable`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A product rule alone: case classes display, sums do not
        code
            import soundness.*
            import dysasymptotics.linearAccess

            trait Display:
              type Self
              def display(value: Self): Text

            object Display extends ProductDerivable[Display]:
              given (Text is Display) = identity(_)
              given (Int is Display) = _.toString.tt

              inline def conjunction[derivation <: Product: ProductReflection]: derivation is Display =
                value =>
                  fields(value):
                    [field] => field => contextual.display(field)
                  . join(t",")

            extension [value](value: value)
              def display(using display: value is Display): Text = display.display(value)

            case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

            sealed trait Contact
            case class Email(address: Text) extends Contact
            case class Phone(number: Text) extends Contact

            Person(t"Juliet", 13).display                     // t"Juliet,13"
            (Email(t"juliet@example.com"): Contact).display   // does not compile
      frame
        label A sum rule as well: the variant's own instance displays it
        code
            import soundness.*
            import dysasymptotics.linearAccess

            trait Display:
              type Self
              def display(value: Self): Text

            object Display extends Derivable[Display]:
              given (Text is Display) = identity(_)
              given (Int is Display) = _.toString.tt

              inline def conjunction[derivation <: Product: ProductReflection]: derivation is Display =
                value =>
                  fields(value):
                    [field] => field => contextual.display(field)
                  . join(t",")

              inline def disjunction[derivation: SumReflection]: derivation is Display =
                value =>
                  variant(value):
                    [variant <: derivation] => variant => contextual.display(variant)

            extension [value](value: value)
              def display(using display: value is Display): Text = display.display(value)

            case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

            sealed trait Contact
            case class Email(address: Text) extends Contact
            case class Phone(number: Text) extends Contact

            Person(t"Juliet", 13).display                     // t"Juliet,13"
            (Email(t"juliet@example.com"): Contact).display   // t"juliet@example.com"
      ##
      ```

      Overrides for one field path come through vicarious: with a `Duel is Specific over
      Display` in scope, built as `specifically { case root.opponent.name() => shout }`, the
      derived instance uses `shout` at exactly that path and the defaults elsewhere.
  refer wisteria.Derivation
  refer wisteria.ProductDerivable

entry .ProductDerivable   `ProductDerivation` for a typeclass written in the `Self` style
    `ProductDerivable[Typeclass]` is `ProductDerivation[[self] =>> Typeclass { type Self =
    self }]`: the product-only derivation for a typeclass whose subject is its `Self` type
    member, with `conjunction` returning `derivation is Typeclass`. See `Derivable` for the
    two-rule form.

entry .Reflection   evidence that a type is a product or a sum: `scala.deriving.Mirror.Of`
    `Reflection[derivation]` is the compiler's `Mirror.Of[derivation]`, the evidence every
    `derived` given takes: it exists for every case class, tuple, enumeration, sealed trait
    and singleton, and for nothing else, which is why a plain trait or a non-case class cannot
    be derived for. A `ProductReflection` satisfies a `Reflection` requirement, since
    `Mirror.ProductOf` is a `Mirror.Of`. A mirror resolves within a nested context-bound
    search, which is what lets a derived instance be summoned from inside another
    typeclass's own derivation, such as a format's `read[T]`.

entry .ProductReflection   evidence that a type is a product: `scala.deriving.Mirror.ProductOf`
    `ProductReflection[derivation <: Product]` is the context bound `conjunction` requires. It
    carries the product's field labels and types, and its `fromProduct` is how `build`
    constructs a value.

entry .SumReflection   evidence that a type is a sum: `scala.deriving.Mirror.SumOf`
    `SumReflection[derivation]` is the context bound `disjunction` requires. It carries the
    variants' labels and types, and its `ordinal` is how `variant` finds the actual case of a
    value.

entry .contextual   the typeclass instance for the current field or variant, inside a combinator's lambda
    Inside the polymorphic lambda given to `fields`, `contexts`, `build`, `construct`,
    `variant`, `delegate` or `choices`, `contextual` is the `typeclass[field]` (or
    `typeclass[variant]`) instance for the current element's type — a hand-written given,
    a codec, or a sibling instance from the same derived graph — and the only means the body
    has of doing anything with a value whose type it cannot see. Most combinators also pass
    the same instance as the lambda's parameter, so `[field] => parsing => parsing.parse(…)`
    and `contextual.parse(…)` are the same instance.

entry .label   the name of the current field or variant, as `Text`, inside a combinator's lambda
    For a field, the name as written in the case class (`_1`, `_2`, … for a tuple); for a
    variant, the case's name. A renderer uses it as a key, a parser matches it against a
    discriminator. In the `soundness` umbrella the name is shared with tarantula's
    `Element#label()`: a file which imports both and finds `label` ambiguous writes
    `wisteria.label`.

entry .index   the position of the current field or variant, inside a combinator's lambda
    For a field, an `Int & FieldIndex[field]`; for a variant, an `Int & Variant.Index[variant]`.
    The brand ties the number to the element's type, so `complement(product)` can read the
    same field out of another value and type it correctly. It compares and arithmetises as an
    `Int`, but indexing a native `List` with it is written `columns(Ordinal.zerary(index))`,
    which yields an `Optional`, under an imported complexity gate such as
    `dysasymptotics.linearAccess`.

entry .dereference   a function from the product to the current field, inside a `contexts` lambda
    `dereference` is a `derivation -> field`: with no value to fold over, `contexts` gives
    each field a way to be read from any value of the product, typed with the field's type.
    Bind it once — `val extract: derivation => field = dereference` — and apply it to each of
    the values a two-value typeclass compares, so both fields reach the one `contextual`
    instance without ever losing their type.

entry .variant   `wisteria.variant` reads the tagged `"variant"` value from context; the combinator is `SumDerivation.Methods#variant`
    The package-level accessor `variant[value]` reads a contextual value tagged `"variant"`,
    in the style of `index` and `label`. The combinator a `disjunction` calls as
    `variant(value) { [variant <: derivation] => variant => … }` is the inherited method of
    `SumDerivation.Methods`, which dispatches on a sum value's actual case; inside the
    deriving object the method shadows this accessor.

entry .arithmetic   field-wise `+`, `-`, `*` and `/` for any product, as givens imported by name
  detail
      Four derivations of symbolism's arithmetic typeclasses, each adding, subtracting,
      multiplying or dividing two values of a product field by field through the fields' own
      instances, so `Text` concatenates while `Int` sums with no instance written for the
      product. Each is a given to import by name — `import soundness.*` does not bring them in
      — because an arithmetic on every case class is a choice, not a default.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import arithmetic.addable

      case class Pair(label: Text, count: Int)

      Pair(t"Mac", 10) + Pair(t"beth", 15)   // Pair(t"Macbeth", 25)
      ```

      The derivation objects (`AddableDerivation` and its siblings) are `ProductDerivation`s
      of the respective typeclass, available for an explicit `derived[T]`, and each given is
      `Derivation.derived[value]` for any `value <: Product` with a `ProductReflection`.
  entry .AddableDerivation   `ProductDerivation` of `value is Addable by value to value`
      Its `conjunction` builds the sum of two products with `build`, adding each pair of
      corresponding fields (read with `complement`) through the field's `Addable`.
  entry .addable   the given: any product is `Addable by` itself `to` itself, field-wise
      `AddableDerivation.derived[value]` for every `value <: Product` with a
      `ProductReflection`; every field type needs its own `Addable`. Imported by name.
  entry .SubtractableDerivation   `ProductDerivation` of `value is Subtractable by value to value`
      As `AddableDerivation`, with each pair of fields subtracted.
  entry .subtractable   the given: any product is `Subtractable by` itself `to` itself, field-wise
      `SubtractableDerivation.derived[value]`; imported by name.
  entry .MultiplicableDerivation   `ProductDerivation` of `value is Multiplicable by value to value`
      As `AddableDerivation`, with each pair of fields multiplied.
  entry .multiplicable   the given: any product is `Multiplicable by` itself `to` itself, field-wise
      `MultiplicableDerivation.derived[value]`; imported by name.
  entry .DivisibleDerivation   `ProductDerivation` of `value is Divisible by value to value`
      As `AddableDerivation`, with each pair of fields divided.
  entry .divisible   the given: any product is `Divisible by` itself `to` itself, field-wise
      `DivisibleDerivation.derived[value]`; imported by name.

entry .Discriminable   how a sum's variant is told apart on the wire, for a format's decoder
  detail
      A format which encodes a sum must say which variant a value is, and a `value is
      Discriminable in Form` says how: `discriminate` reads the variant's label out of a value
      in the format's `Form` (a JSON node, say), `variant` strips the discriminator to leave
      the variant's own payload, and `rewrite` adds a label to a payload for encoding. The
      typeclass is defined here, next to the derivation which consults it, and implemented by
      each format: jacinta's `discriminables` package offers a `type` field, a `kind` field, a
      wrapper object keyed by the variant's name and a `type`/`value` envelope, each a given
      imported by name. A format's derived decoder calls `discriminate` and then `delegate`s
      to the variant it names, raising `Variant.Error` for an unknown label.
  entry #rewrite   a payload in the form, with the variant's label `kind` written into it
  entry #discriminate   the variant label a value in the form carries, or `Unset` if it has none
  entry #variant   the variant's own payload, with the discriminator removed

entry .FieldIndex   the brand on a field's `index`: `Int & FieldIndex[field]`
    A sealed, uninhabited trait intersected with `Int` to tie a field's position to its type,
    so that `complement(product)`, given the index, can read the field out of another value
    and return it as `field`. The `index` accessor yields it inside a product combinator's
    lambda; it is never constructed by hand.

entry .Variant   the vocabulary of sum derivation: a variant's `Index`, and the `Error` for an unknown label
  detail
      There is no `Variant` type: a variant is a case of the sum being derived, identified by
      its position (`Variant.Index`) and its label. The object holds the error a producer
      raises when a label names no variant, and the brand on a variant's index.
  refer wisteria.SumDerivation.Methods
  entry .Error   raised when a label names no variant of the sum: _the specified variant (`x`) is not one of the valid variants (…) of sum type `S`_
    detail
        Raised, through a `Tactic[Variant.Error]`, by `delegate` and `singleton` when the
        label they are given — usually a discriminator read from a decoder's input — matches
        none of the sum's variants. The message lists the valid variants, so the error says
        what the input should have carried. A derivation which calls either combinator needs
        the tactic in scope where it expands: `provide[Tactic[Variant.Error]]` inside the
        instance keeps the derived instance from capturing the caller's own.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        trait Parsing[value]:
          def parse(text: Text): value

        object Parsing extends Derivation[Parsing]:
          given Parsing[Int] = _.s.toInt
          given Parsing[Boolean] = _ == t"yes"

          inline def conjunction[derivation <: Product: ProductReflection]: Parsing[derivation] =
            text => build[derivation] { [field] => parsing => parsing.parse(text) }

          inline def disjunction[derivation: SumReflection]: Parsing[derivation] =
            text =>
              text.cut(t":") match
                case List(prefix: Text, rest: Text) =>
                  provide[Tactic[Variant.Error]]:
                    delegate[derivation](prefix):
                      [variant <: derivation] => parsing => parsing.parse(rest)

        sealed trait Wrapped
        case class WrappedInt(value: Int) extends Wrapped
        case class WrappedBool(value: Boolean) extends Wrapped

        try Parsing.derived[Wrapped].parse(t"Unknown:42").toString.tt
        catch case error: Variant.Error => error.validVariants.join(t", ")   // t"WrappedInt, WrappedBool"
        ```

        `Variant.Error[derivation](label)` builds the error for a sum type, filling in the
        sum's name and its variant labels at compile time.
    entry .apply   builds the error for a sum type: `Variant.Error[Sum](label)`
        An inline method which fills in `sum` and `validVariants` from the type's symbol, so a
        caller supplies only the offending label.
    entry #inputLabel   the label which named no variant
    entry #sum   the name of the sum type
    entry #validVariants   the labels of the sum's variants, in declaration order
  entry .Index   the brand on a variant's `index`: `Int & Variant.Index[variant]`
    detail
        A sealed, uninhabited trait intersected with `Int` to tie a variant's position to its
        type. The `index` accessor yields it inside a sum combinator's lambda, and
        `complement(sum)` uses it to narrow another value to the same variant.
    entry .apply   brands an `Int` as the index of a variant: `Variant.Index[variant](int)`

entry .internal   the macros behind the derivation engine and the combinators
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object houses the macro implementations the inline combinators
      splice, and the accessors tests and tooling use. `derived` is `deriveGraph`; `fields`,
      `contexts`, `build` and `construct` are `fieldsProduct`, `contextsProduct`,
      `buildProduct` and `constructMonadic`; `choice` is `isChoice`. `fieldInstance` is the
      implicit search which resolves a field's instance while ignoring the typeclass's own
      derivation givens, so a field lands on a hand-written given, a codec or a sibling of the
      graph and never re-enters the derivation.
  entry .default   a field's declared default value, by its index, or `Unset`
  entry .sumName   the name of a type, as `Text`, from its symbol
  entry .variantLabelList   the labels of a sum's variants, in declaration order
  entry .fieldLabelList   the labels of a product's fields, in declaration order
  entry .field   the typeclass instance for an element, resolved without re-entering the derivation
  entry .overridePaths   the dotted field paths a `Specific over typeclass` in scope overrides, or `Nil`
  entry .overridePathsMacro   the macro behind `overridePaths`
  entry .Reentrant   a marker given during a re-entry probe, so a type reaching itself resolves to its sibling
  entry .reentrySentinel   what a re-entry probe returns; never evaluated
  entry .isSum   whether a type is a sum, decided as the code compiles
  entry .isSumMacro   the macro behind `isSum`
  entry .fieldLabels   the macro behind `fieldLabelList`
  entry .isSumType   whether a type representation is a sealed trait or enumeration with children
  entry .isProductType   whether a type representation is a product the engine can build
  entry .isChoice   the macro behind `choice`: whether every variant is a singleton
  entry .fieldInstance   the macro behind `field`
  entry .deriveGraph   the macro behind `derived`: one lazily bound instance per reachable type
  entry .buildProduct   the macro behind `build`
  entry .contextsProduct   the macro behind `contexts`
  entry .fieldsProduct   the macro behind `fields`
  entry .constructMonadic   the macro behind `construct`
  entry .typeName   the macro behind `sumName`
  entry .variantLabels   the macro behind `variantLabelList`
  entry .getDefault   the macro behind `default`: reads the synthetic default-argument method
